WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.602
[chime]

00:00:02.669 --> 00:00:03.970
[beeping]

00:00:04.037 --> 00:00:06.940
- Off the coast
of North Carolina

00:00:07.006 --> 00:00:09.109
lies a treacherous
stretch of water

00:00:09.175 --> 00:00:13.213
with strong currents
and shifting sands.

00:00:13.279 --> 00:00:16.316
With the remains of
up to 5,000 ships,

00:00:16.383 --> 00:00:20.320
it's known as the
graveyard of the Atlantic.

00:00:20.387 --> 00:00:24.224
Hidden within this eerie
resting place for lost souls

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:28.595
are gangs of Sand Tiger sharks.

00:00:28.661 --> 00:00:32.465
Photographer and conservationist
Tanya Houppermans

00:00:32.532 --> 00:00:35.335
has spent over a decade
documenting the shark

00:00:35.402 --> 00:00:37.270
gang phenomenon occurring here.

00:00:37.337 --> 00:00:39.606
- I started diving around 2009.

00:00:39.672 --> 00:00:41.307
Came down here to
North Carolina,

00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:45.812
and that was the first time that
I saw the Sand Tiger sharks.

00:00:45.879 --> 00:00:47.847
It literally changed my life.

00:00:47.914 --> 00:00:49.616
I walked into work and quit.

00:00:49.682 --> 00:00:51.351
I left the corporate
world behind

00:00:51.418 --> 00:00:54.087
and decided to devote my life
to spending time with sharks.

00:00:54.154 --> 00:00:56.790
- Working with Spot
A Shark USA, Tanya

00:00:56.856 --> 00:00:59.392
is studying the thousands
of sharks that have made

00:00:59.459 --> 00:01:01.361
these shipwrecks their home.

00:01:01.428 --> 00:01:04.497
These rusting hulks provide
the perfect conditions

00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:09.369
for marine life to grow
and for predators to hunt.

00:01:09.436 --> 00:01:12.038
But why Sand Tiger
sharks congregate here

00:01:12.105 --> 00:01:15.909
in such large gangs
is still a mystery.

00:01:15.975 --> 00:01:18.778
- There are times that we could
see a hundred and maybe even

00:01:18.845 --> 00:01:20.680
more sharks in an aggregation.

00:01:20.747 --> 00:01:24.284
They really seem to prefer to be
around other Sand Tiger sharks.

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:26.319
We're not quite sure why yet.

00:01:26.386 --> 00:01:29.189
- Today, Tanya and
her husband Scott

00:01:29.255 --> 00:01:31.691
are looking for answers
by diving a wreck

00:01:31.758 --> 00:01:35.228
known for large groups
of Sand Tiger sharks.

00:01:35.295 --> 00:01:37.530
- Today were heading out to
the wreck of the Caribsea.

00:01:37.597 --> 00:01:40.033
This is a freighter that
was sunk by a u-boat

00:01:40.099 --> 00:01:41.768
during World War II.

00:01:41.835 --> 00:01:46.439
Right now, she lies
about 90 feet underwater.

00:01:46.506 --> 00:01:48.208
That's where we tend
to see a lot of Sand

00:01:48.274 --> 00:01:49.976
Tiger sharks hanging out.

00:01:50.043 --> 00:01:53.913
- Tanya is on the front line,
trying to discover the secret

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:55.715
that unites these sharks.

00:01:55.782 --> 00:01:58.384
She's taking photographs
so scientists

00:01:58.451 --> 00:02:02.322
can study them for vital clues.

00:02:02.388 --> 00:02:04.224
- Inside this housing
is the camera that I

00:02:04.290 --> 00:02:06.392
use to photograph the sharks.

00:02:06.459 --> 00:02:09.229
And we have two lasers
here on each side.

00:02:09.295 --> 00:02:11.397
When I take a
picture of a shark,

00:02:11.464 --> 00:02:14.968
these two green dots will show
up on the side of the shark.

00:02:15.034 --> 00:02:17.804
We can then actually calculate
the length of that shark

00:02:17.871 --> 00:02:19.906
just from the photograph itself.

00:02:19.973 --> 00:02:21.441
And then we can
go in and identify

00:02:21.508 --> 00:02:24.477
each individual shark based
on its unique spot patterns.

00:02:24.544 --> 00:02:28.481
It's like a fingerprint
is to a person.

00:02:28.548 --> 00:02:32.352
I want to kind of give each
other's gear a look over, too.

00:02:32.418 --> 00:02:33.753
[puff]

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:34.687
All right.

00:02:34.754 --> 00:02:35.622
Looks good.

00:02:35.688 --> 00:02:37.156
Every dive, there's
the possibility

00:02:37.223 --> 00:02:39.392
of learning something
new about these animals.

00:02:39.459 --> 00:02:42.462
There really hasn't been a
lot of research done on them,

00:02:42.529 --> 00:02:44.731
compared to a lot of other
species such as Great Whites,

00:02:44.797 --> 00:02:46.699
so any time we get in
the water with them

00:02:46.766 --> 00:02:48.968
and bring back any information
for the researchers

00:02:49.035 --> 00:02:51.504
that I work with,
it's a great day.

00:02:51.571 --> 00:02:53.039
Good, then.

00:02:53.106 --> 00:02:54.107
Right.

00:02:54.173 --> 00:02:55.675
Let's go find some sharks.

00:02:58.611 --> 00:03:00.513
[splash]

00:03:00.580 --> 00:03:02.782
[air hissing]

00:03:02.849 --> 00:03:05.184
[air hissing]

00:03:05.251 --> 00:03:07.687
We have 100 feet of
visibility, clear blue water.

00:03:10.723 --> 00:03:14.427
A lot of the wreckage
still intact--

00:03:14.494 --> 00:03:17.230
you can see the
big bow structure.

00:03:17.297 --> 00:03:18.898
It never gets old.

00:03:18.965 --> 00:03:24.037
There are just sharks as far
as I can see all around me.

00:03:24.103 --> 00:03:26.940
- These sharks are named
after tigers because

00:03:27.006 --> 00:03:28.474
of their voracious appetite.

00:03:28.541 --> 00:03:32.478
But to Tanya, they're
gentle giants.

00:03:32.545 --> 00:03:34.480
- They have these
raggedy teeth that

00:03:34.547 --> 00:03:37.317
makes them look really
fierce, but these sharks

00:03:37.383 --> 00:03:38.985
are full of personality.

00:03:39.052 --> 00:03:42.889
Sand Tiger sharks are probably
about six or seven feet,

00:03:42.956 --> 00:03:46.292
the female being a little
bit bigger than the male.

00:03:46.359 --> 00:03:49.062
- Surrounded by a
swarm of sharks,

00:03:49.128 --> 00:03:51.798
Tanya begins her
search for evidence

00:03:51.864 --> 00:03:53.700
as to why so many are here.

00:03:57.070 --> 00:03:59.505
- There are a lot of females.

00:03:59.572 --> 00:04:04.444
Every so often, you see a few
males, but it's mostly females.

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:06.379
Why is that?

00:04:06.446 --> 00:04:10.717
- With her laser guided camera,
Tanya captures her first clue

00:04:10.783 --> 00:04:12.385
to solving this mystery.

00:04:16.222 --> 00:04:20.059
- Some of the females do
have scars from mating.

00:04:20.126 --> 00:04:23.296
When sharks mate, the
male actually holds

00:04:23.363 --> 00:04:26.599
on the female with his teeth.

00:04:26.666 --> 00:04:32.071
If we see fresh mating scars,
that female may be pregnant.

00:04:32.138 --> 00:04:36.009
We're finding several
pregnant females.

00:04:36.075 --> 00:04:37.877
Why are they grouping together?

00:04:42.015 --> 00:04:44.183
- After photographing
the girl gang--

00:04:44.250 --> 00:04:45.351
- That was amazing.

00:04:45.418 --> 00:04:47.020
There was a lot of sharks.

00:04:47.086 --> 00:04:51.524
- Tanya tries to make sense
of this incredible spectacle.

00:04:51.591 --> 00:04:55.094
- So what we're seeing is a
big gang of Sand Tiger sharks,

00:04:55.161 --> 00:04:57.130
mostly females.

00:04:57.196 --> 00:04:59.132
We have so many
unanswered questions.

00:04:59.198 --> 00:05:01.501
But what we're thinking is
that maybe these females

00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:04.704
are aggregating together
during pregnancy

00:05:04.771 --> 00:05:07.306
so that the males don't try
to mate with them again.

00:05:07.373 --> 00:05:10.877
It could be possible that the
females are banding together

00:05:10.943 --> 00:05:13.046
as kind of a support network.

00:05:13.112 --> 00:05:16.149
They are choosing to
group together like this.

00:05:16.215 --> 00:05:22.422
And so not all sharks
are just solo predators.

00:05:22.488 --> 00:05:25.925
Sharks could have a social
life, just like humans.

00:05:25.992 --> 00:05:29.195
[gentle music]

00:05:29.262 --> 00:05:32.198
[gentle music]

00:05:32.265 --> 00:05:35.201
[gentle music]

00:05:35.268 --> 00:05:37.704
[gentle music]

00:05:37.770 --> 00:05:39.906
[gentle music]

